{"records":[{"id":"xn3vv2cdnk","title":"High-throughput CRISPR screens to dissect macrophage-Shigella interactions","description":"The positive hits, negative hits, and non-targeting controls of genome-wide CRISPR screens; Supplemental Tables.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-07-23T06:18:30.182Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/xn3vv2cdnk","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/xn3vv2cdnk"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yong","last_name":"LAI","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Genetic Screening","Host-Pathogen Interaction","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"hfbhym45fz","title":"CRISPR screening reveals a requirement for ribosome recycling in efficient SARS-CoV-2 programmed ribosomal frameshifting and viral replication. Rehfeld et al.","description":"Supplemental Table S1. Results of SARS-CoV-2 programmed ribosomal frameshifting genome-wide CRISPR screens (MAGeCK analysis).\nSupplemental Table S2. Oligonucleotides.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-01-19T12:17:58.922Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/hfbhym45fz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/hfbhym45fz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Joshua","last_name":"Mendell","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Frederick","last_name":"Rehfeld","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/0003xa228","name":"Cancer Prevention and Research Institute of Texas","grant_id":"RP220309"},{"id":"https://ror.org/00np6vq88","name":"Welch Foundation","grant_id":"I-1961-20210327"},{"id":"https://ror.org/006w34k90","name":"Howard Hughes Medical Institute","grant_id":null}],"categories":["Oligonucleotide","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"k6wm46g4tw","title":"Data set for the paper entitled \"CRISPR-enabled genetic screens identify synthetic lethal targets across frequently altered cancer drivers\" by Desjardins, Bowlan, et al. (2026)","description":"We undertook a series of genome-wide CRISPR screens using functionally validated isogenic cell lines and mapped SL interactions across 15 genetic alterations characteristic of diseases with high incidence and unmet clinical need: FBXW7, CCNE1, CDK12, ARID1A, KMT2D, DNMT3A, TET2, KEAP1, STK11, IDH1, SF3B1, SRSF2, U2AF1, chromosome 18q loss, and chromosome 13q loss. The data deposited here make the raw read count files available for all CRISPR screens conducted in the study. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-01-26T11:43:11.208Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/k6wm46g4tw","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/k6wm46g4tw"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jordan","last_name":"Young","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alejandro","last_name":"Álvarez-Quilón","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jessica","last_name":"Desjardins","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Julian","last_name":"Bowlan","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Primary Data"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9x47wzy8x7","title":"A study of \"Genome-wide CRISPR screens reveal host factors critical for SARS-CoV-2 infection\" by Wei et al. Cell 2020","description":"Identification of host genes essential for SARS-CoV-2 infection may reveal novel therapeutic targets and inform our understanding of COVID-19 pathogenesis. Here, we performed genome-wide CRISPR screens in Vero-E6 cells with SARS-CoV-2, MERS-CoV, bat coronavirus HKU5 expressing the SARS-CoV-1 spike, and VSV expressing the SARS-CoV-2 spike. Together this identifies potential therapeutic targets for SARS-CoV-2 and reveals SARS-lineage specific and pan-coronavirus host factors that regulate susceptibility to highly pathogenic coronaviruses.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-10-15T21:29:14.06Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9x47wzy8x7","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9x47wzy8x7"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Craig","last_name":"Wilen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["CRISPR","Middle East Respiratory Syndrome-Related Coronavirus","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2","COVID-19"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"rhsw3nbjhz","title":"Essential gene profiles for human pluripotent stem cells identify uncharacterized genes and substrate dependencies. Mair et al.","description":"Human pluripotent stem cells (hPSCs) provide an invaluable tool for modeling diseases and hold promise for regenerative medicine. For understanding pluripotency and lineage differentiation mechanisms, a critical first step involves systematically cataloging genes that are indispensable for hPSC maintenance and proliferation. To map genetic determinants of hPSC fitness, we performed genome-scale loss-of-function screens in an inducible Cas9 H1 hPSC line cultured on feeder cells and feeder-free to identify essential genes. Among these, we found FOXH1 and VENTX, genes that encode transcription factors previously implicated in stem cell biology, as well as an uncharacterized gene, C22orf43/DRICH1. hPSCs essential genes are substantially different from other cell lines, and gene essentiality in hPSCs is highly context-dependent with respect to different growth substrates. Our CRISPR screens establish parameters for genome-wide screens in hPSCs, which will facilitate the characterization of unappreciated regulators of hPSC biology to understand the genetic wiring of hPSC fitness and pluripotency.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-01-24T11:57:50.932Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/rhsw3nbjhz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/rhsw3nbjhz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Barbara","last_name":"Mair","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jelena","last_name":"Tomic","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sanna","last_name":"Masud","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jason","last_name":"Moffat","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Stem Cell","Functional Genomics","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"z4ht3txn2k","title":"Accompanying Files for paper entitled: \"Genome-scale mapping of DNA damage suppressors through phenotypic CRISPR/Cas9 screens\" - Zhao et al. Molecular Cell 2023","description":"Accompanying Files for paper entitled: \"Genome-scale mapping of DNA damage suppressors through phenotypic CRISPR/Cas9 screens\" - Zhao et al. Molecular Cell 2023. This dataset contains Source and additional Data for all figures.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-06-06T07:16:19.286Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/z4ht3txn2k","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/z4ht3txn2k"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Daniel","last_name":"Durocher","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yichao","last_name":"Zhao","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01gavpb45","name":"Canadian Institutes of Health Research","grant_id":null}],"categories":["DNA Replication","DNA Repair","Flow Cytometry","Cancer Chemotherapy","DNA Damage","Functional Genomics","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"nxvdzybwhf","title":"Illuminating host-mycobacterial interactions with genome-wide CRISPR knockout and CRISPRi screens","description":"The data deposited here contains genome-wide CRISPR knockout and CRISPRi screen data, including the positive hits, the negative hits, and non-targeting controls. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-08-30T11:32:01.108Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/nxvdzybwhf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/nxvdzybwhf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yong","last_name":"Lai","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Functional Genomics","High-Throughput Sequencing","Tuberculosis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9w4g54bv43","title":"Genome-wide screens reveal that resveratrol induces replicative stress in human cells","description":"Resveratrol is a natural product associated with wide-ranging effects in animal and cellular models including lifespan extension. To identify the genetic target of resveratrol in human cells, we conducted genome-wide CRISPR-Cas9 screens to pinpoint genes that confer sensitivity or resistance to resveratrol. An extensive network of DNA damage response and replicative stress genes exhibited genetic interactions with resveratrol and its analog pterostilbene. These genetic profiles showed similarity to the response to hydroxyurea, an inhibitor of ribonucleotide reductase that causes replicative stress. Resveratrol, pterostilbene and hydroxyurea caused similar depletion of nucleotide pools, inhibition of replication fork progression and induction of replicative stress. The ability of resveratrol to inhibit cell proliferation and S phase transit was independent of the histone deacetylase Sirtuin 1, which has been implicated in lifespan extension by resveratrol. These results establish that a primary impact of resveratrol on human cell proliferation is the induction of low-level replicative stress.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-08-04T14:56:19.488Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9w4g54bv43","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9w4g54bv43"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yahya","last_name":"Benslimane","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Thierry","last_name":"Bertomeu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jasmin","last_name":"Coulombe-Huntington","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mary","last_name":"McQuaid","orcid":null},{"first_name":"María","last_name":"Sánchez-Osuna","orcid":null},{"first_name":"David","last_name":"Papadopoli","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Daina","last_name":"Avizonis","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mariana","last_name":"De Sa Tavares Russo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Caroline","last_name":"Huard","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ivan","last_name":"Topisirovic","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Hugo","last_name":"Wurtele","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mike","last_name":"Tyers","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lea","last_name":"Harrington","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["DNA Replication","High-Throughput Screening","High-Throughput Sequencing"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"s3jtdhjdbs","title":"Supplementary data for Michaelis et al., 'Protein import motor complex reacts to mitochondrial misfolding by reducing protein import and activating mitophagy'","description":"Supplementary data for Michaelis et al., 'Mitochondrial import loss induces mitophagy without membrane depolarization'. We hypothesized that inhibition of mitochondrial protein import induced mitophagy independent of mitochondrial depolarization. This dataset contains raw Illumina sequencing data for all samples from a genome-wide CRISPR/Cas9 screen for mitophagy regulators. The genome-wide library is Brunello from Doench et al Nat Biotechnol. 2016. We found that the pre-sequence translocase-associated motor complex PAM controls mitophagy induction. Loss of PAM caused a loss in protein import and was sufficient to induce mitophagy without depolarization.\nThe samples have been sequenced on an Illumina NextSeq 500 in single end mode with 75+8 cycles. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-08-05T15:38:53.752Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/s3jtdhjdbs","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/s3jtdhjdbs"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jonas","last_name":"Michaelis","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Melinda","last_name":"Brunstein","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Süleyman","last_name":"Bozkurt","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ludovico","last_name":"Alves","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Martin","last_name":"Wegner","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Manuel","last_name":"Kaulich","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Christian","last_name":"Pohl","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Christian","last_name":"Münch","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Proteomics","Mitochondrion","Mitophagy","Genetic Screening","Next Generation Sequencing","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"f7btcr6x6d","title":"A non-canonical function of glutathione to suppress ferroptosis via FSP1","description":"Whole genome-wide CRISPR KO screen","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-10-02T11:41:52.925Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/f7btcr6x6d","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/f7btcr6x6d"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"BO","last_name":"CHU","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":31745,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":56,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":31745,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":31677,"restricted":24,"embargoed":44},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":583},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":265},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":115},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":114},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":105},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":100},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":100},{"id":"https://ror.org/00djwmt25","name":"University Grants Committee","count":99},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":96},{"id":"h